																		Author Commentary
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/theglyphstone" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1359260); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1359260&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608411081" alt="TheGlyphstone" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1359260)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/theglyphstone" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1359260); return false;" >TheGlyphstone</a></span> <span class="odate time_1336706046 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">11 May 2012 03:14</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1445106">
									

<p>My first SCP that I'm willing to admit to/that made it off my sandbox page.</p>
<p>Basic idea seed - your reflection is one of the most mundane things ever - utterly predictable, utterly controllable&#8230;so what if it wasn't? What if your reflection wasn't actually your reflection, but a <em>something else</em> pretending to be your reflection - and the longer it stayed with you, the worse and worse it got at keeping up pretenses?</p>
<p>The weakest point I can see, as pointed out to me in the IRC, is [Redacting] the visual description of SCP-1620-1 in its Stage 3. Hiding the horror might be a crutch for new/lazy writers, but I just can't think of anything really solid that fits with the article's mood while still being scary.</p>
<p>I'm aware of SCP-919, the closest existing article in theme to this, but I think I'm sufficiently differentiated that my 'thing what lives in mirrors' stands on its own.</p>
<p>&#8230;That's it, I guess. Tear it apart, people.</p>

							</div>

								
					
