			<div class="content" id="post-content-1445355">
									

<p>Well, still positive ratings, that's a good sign. Okay, issues to fix&#8230;</p>
<p>The [REDACTED] description: If it really is dragging down the article, I can try to put something in there, but I really don't know what. Lovecraftian tentacle monsters are stupid, and the only other thing I could come up with would be a 50's-sci-fi-Grey alien, which is both not-scary and too much emphasis on 'SCP-1620 is FROM SPAAAAAACE', which I don't want explicit at all. I'll try and brainstorm some suitably horrific visages and bounce them off people.</p>
<p>Test Logs: If they feel like padding, I can shunt them behind a collapsible tag or dump them to a sub-page. I'm loathe to just remove them entirely right now, though, because I felt they answered some - not all, but some - important questions about how 1620 and 1620-1 acted without clumsy exposition dumping. Really, the log I was most uncertain on was the last one, where the two creatures fight in the mirror and one of the hosts dies; it shows that separate instances of 1620-1 are individuals, and territorial/solitary 'hunters', and gives a bit of teeth to the need to keep 1620 contained, but it's also breaking the previously consistent rule of 'the mirror-entities can't actually affect the outside world', which could be good or bad.</p>
<p>Containment wording: Noted, that'll be easy to patch.</p>
<p>@Khym: Actually&#8230;I like that idea a lot. I'll need some time to figure out how/where to implement it, but I think it fits rather well with the progression of infection - at first it tries to pretend everything's normal to the host, then gives up and admits it's independent but still tries to be nice, and only in maturity does it drop all pretenses because it doesn't need them docile anymore.</p>

